00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:31.610
<v Mormon Missionary 2>Right. Our message is that God has a prophet on the earth again.
<v Mormon Missionary 2>对。我们的信息是上帝再次在地球上有了先知。

00:43:31.610 --> 00:43:35.060
<v Man>There are many prophets.
<v Man>先知有很多。

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:39.090
<v Mormon Missionary 2>This is true. But there are those ordained, like Moses.
<v Mormon Missionary 2>确实。但有一些是受过圣职的，像摩西那样。

00:43:39.090 --> 00:43:54.390
<v Jane Feltes>It was like watching a couple of football fans talk about the Superbowl, even though they were rooting for different teams. They know they'll never convince the other that their team is better. But they're really enjoying the back and forth. They seem relieved to have found someone who relates to the world the way they do.
<v Jane Feltes>这就像看几个橄榄球迷讨论超级碗，尽管他们支持不同的球队。他们知道永远无法说服对方他们的球队更好。但他们真的很享受这种往返交流。他们似乎很高兴找到了一个看待世界方式相同的人。

00:43:54.390 --> 00:43:55.810
<v Man>God bless you.
<v Man>上帝保佑你。

00:43:55.810 --> 00:44:03.650
<v Mormon Missionary 2>You too, brother. Have a good one.
<v Mormon Missionary 2>你也是，弟兄。祝你有愉快的一天。

00:44:03.650 --> 00:44:18.350
<v Mormon Missionary 2>I think some of my favorite people I like talking to, I like talking to Jews and I like talking to Muslims. Muslims are really cool just because I find that they're more devout. It's easier for me to talk to them because they're very faithful people.
<v Mormon Missionary 2>我觉得我最喜欢交谈的人是我喜欢和犹太人谈，我也喜欢和穆斯林谈。穆斯林真的很酷，因为我发现他们更虔诚。和他们谈话更容易，因为他们是非常忠诚的人。

00:44:18.350 --> 00:44:19.620
<v Jane Feltes>You guys have that in common.
<v Jane Feltes>你们有共同点。

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:38.080
<v Mormon Missionary 1>Yeah, that's true. But like going along with the Jews and Muslims and stuff is they understand the idea of the covenant, you know, a two-way agreement. So they're more devout with it.
<v Mormon Missionary 1>是的，是真的。但就像和犹太人和穆斯林相处，他们理解契约的概念，你知道，双向协议。所以他们对此更虔诚。

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:56.930
<v Jane Feltes>At 6:00 PM it's dark and so cold nobody's on the street anymore. So the guys head off on a home visit to meet with a new potential convert. They have three hours of work ahead of them today, and roughly a combined 3,240 hours to go.
<v Jane Feltes>下午6点，天黑了，天气冷得街上没人了。所以他们去进行一次家访，去见一个潜在的新教徒。他们今天还有三个小时的工作，总共还需要大约3240个小时。

00:44:56.930 --> 00:45:04.510
<v Ira Glass>Jane Feltes is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>Jane Feltes是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:45:04.510 --> 00:45:16.530
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Contrails of My Tears. We end our show today with this story of a place that is the exact opposite of a tough room. Brett Martin says it's happened him.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，“我泪水的凝结尾迹”。我们今天以一个与艰难场合完全相反的地方的故事结束我们的节目。Brett Martin说他遇上过。

00:45:16.530 --> 00:45:31.530
<v Brett Martin>A couple of years ago I was on a flight from New York to San Juan, Puerto Rico. The movie was Sweet Home Alabama, which you'll remember. It's about a southern girl played by Reese Witherspoon who moves to New York, joins the fashion industry, and then is forced to return home and come to terms with her white trash roots.
<v Brett Martin>几年前，我乘飞机从纽约飞往波多黎各的圣胡安。电影是《情归阿拉巴马》，你可能还记得。它是关于一个由Reese Witherspoon扮演的南方女孩，她搬到纽约，加入时尚界，然后被迫回家并面对她那种“白垃圾”根源。

00:45:31.530 --> 00:45:39.200
<v Brett Martin>At the end, there's a wedding scene when the character has to explain to her big city husband-to-be that she's leaving him for her earthy, down-home high school sweetheart.
<v Brett Martin>结尾时，有一个婚礼现场，角色必须向她那个大城市的未婚夫解释她为了那个土里土气的家乡青梅竹马而抛弃他。

00:45:39.200 --> 00:46:00.170
<v Reese Witherspoon>The truth is I gave my heart away a long time ago, my whole heart. And I never really got it back. And I don't even know what else to say. But I'm sorry. I can't marry you.
<v Reese Witherspoon>真相是我很久以前就把心交出去了，我的整颗心。我再也没真正拿回来。我不知道还能说什么。我很抱歉。我不能嫁给你。

00:46:00.170 --> 00:46:08.470
<v Brett Martin>Into the stunned silence that follows walks Candice Bergen as the jilted fiance's dragon lady of a mother, who, coincidentally, also happens to be the mayor of New York.
<v Brett Martin>在那随后的惊愕寂静中，Candice Bergen走了进来，扮演那个被抛弃未婚夫的“龙女”母亲，顺便说一下，她碰巧还是纽约市市长。

00:46:08.470 --> 00:46:43.190
<v Brett Martin>After a volley of insults, Witherspoon decks Bergen. And it is at this moment, somewhere between when Witherspoon drawled, "Nobody talks to my mama like that," and her father, Earl Smooter, raised his face to the heavens and declared, "Praise the Lord. The South has rise again," that something because to happen to me. My face got hot and constricted. A softball rose in my throat that required a surprisingly loud snort to choke back. My breathing grew rapid. In short, I lost it and started to cry.
<v Brett Martin>在一阵侮辱之后，Witherspoon把Bergen放倒了。就在这一刻，就在Witherspoon拖着长腔说“没人敢那样跟我妈说话”，而她的父亲Earl Smooter抬起脸对天宣布“赞美主，南方再次崛起”之间，某种事情发生在我身上。我的脸变得灼热且紧缩。我的喉咙里涌起一个肿块，需要一声相当响的吸鼻声才能忍回去。我的呼吸变得急促。简而言之，我崩溃了，开始哭泣。

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:56.940
<v Brett Martin>I should say that Sweet Home Alabama is not a very good movie. It's actually a pretty terrible movie. I have no particular attachment to Reese Witherspoon, and I'm not from the South. Also, this was the fourth time I'd seen it.
<v Brett Martin>我应该说《情归阿拉巴马》不是一部好电影。实际上它是一部相当糟糕的电影。我对Reese Witherspoon没什么特别的依恋，我也不是南方人。而且这是我第四次看它了。

00:46:56.940 --> 00:47:25.330
<v Brett Martin>See, my name is Brett and I cry at movies on airplanes. Not sometimes, always. And not some movies, all movies. Don't believe me? Here's a by no means complete list. Bend It Like Beckham , 101 Dalmations , What A Girl Wants , Daredevil.
<v Brett Martin>看，我叫Brett，我在飞机上看电影会哭。不是有时，总是。不是某些电影，所有电影。不信我？这里有一份绝对不完整的清单：《我爱贝克汉姆》、《101忠狗》、《少女的奇幻旅程》、《夜魔侠》。

00:47:25.330 --> 00:47:49.090
<v Brett Martin>Let me be clear. I am not afraid of flying. I like flying. And I'm not a crier, at least not on land. Like many men I know, even sensitive ones who know that having a cry can be healthy and good, I passed some invisible line in adolescence where I simply stopped doing it. There have been many times in life that I probably should have cried, actually tried to cry, and wasn't able to. Because, of course, I didn't happen to be at 30,000 feet.
<v Brett Martin>说明白点。我不怕坐飞机。我喜欢坐飞机。而且我不是个爱哭的人，至少在陆地上不是。像我认识的许多男人一样，即使是那些知道哭一场是健康且好事的情感敏感男人，我在青春期跨过了一条无形的线，就停止了那样做。生活中有很多次我本来应该哭，实际上试图哭，却哭不出来。因为当然，我没碰巧在3万英尺的高空。

00:47:49.090 --> 00:48:00.070
<v Brett Martin>Needless to say, this can be embarrassing. I once confessed my problem to a friend, and he thought for a long moment before saying, I'm sorry to hear that. Does it make your mascara run?
<v Brett Martin>不用说，这可能很尴尬。我曾经向一个朋友坦白我的问题，他思考了很久才说，很遗憾听到这个。这会让你的睫毛膏花掉吗？

00:48:00.070 --> 00:48:33.530
<v Brett Martin>Earlier this year I was flying from Denver to New York and found myself seated next to a big, burly guy with a cowboy shirt and a western belt buckle. Before takeoff, we talked about football or college basketball or something. Then they announced the movie. It was Under The Tuscan Sun . I glanced at my macho new buddy, thought about watching Diane Lane experience love and loss while rediscovering her inner strength at a farmhouse in the Italian countryside, and read the Sky Mall catalog instead.
<v Brett Martin>今年早些时候我从丹佛飞往纽约，发现自己坐在一个有着牛仔衫和西部皮带扣的魁梧大个子旁边。起飞前，我们聊了足球、大学篮球之类的。然后他们宣布了电影。是《托斯卡纳艳阳下》。我瞥了一眼我那魁梧的新哥们，考虑要不要看Diane Lane在意大利乡村的农舍里重拾内心力量的过程中体验爱情与失去，于是我读起了天际购物目录。

00:48:33.530 --> 00:48:47.320
<v Brett Martin>For a long time, I thought I was alone in this. Then a few months ago, I was at a party and overheard another guest describe how he fell to pieces watching an episode of Everybody Loves Raymond on a flight to California. I started asking around and found I wasn't completely alone.
<v Brett Martin>很长一段时间里，我以为只有我一个人这样。几个月前，我在一个聚会上无意中听到另一位客人描述他在去加州的航班上看着《人人都爱雷蒙德》崩溃的情景。我开始打听，发现我并不孤单。

00:48:47.320 --> 00:48:52.180
<v Brett Martin>Greg is a 32-year-old guy in jeans and a Mets hat who just finished reading a book about college sports.
<v Brett Martin>Greg是一个32岁的家伙，穿着牛仔裤，戴着大都会队的帽子，刚读完一本关于大学体育的书。

00:48:52.180 --> 00:49:19.540
<v Greg>I think it might have been the only movie available was Dirty Dancing 2: Havana Nights. The parents watch them dance and they see how special this relationship is. And at that moment, they've gone from angry parents to sort of accepting of Javier. I mean, I got choked up.
<v Greg>我想可能当时唯一能看的电影是《辣身舞2：哈瓦那之夜》。父母看着他们跳舞，他们看到了这段关系是多么特别。就在那一刻，他们从愤怒的父母转变为接受Javier。我是说，我哽咽了。

00:49:19.540 --> 00:49:24.650
<v Brett Martin>As my fellow weepers will tell you, even not watching the movie is no guarantee of safety. Here's my friend, Lindsay.
<v Brett Martin>正如我的同伴们会告诉你的，即使不看电影也不是安全的保证。这是我的朋友Lindsay。

00:49:24.650 --> 00:50:03.470
<v Lindsay>So I was on a flight, I believe California to New York. The specifics don't really stand out. But I do remember that it required a $2 deposit for earphones or something. And I wasn't ready to pay the $2 or I didn't have $2. And I decided I'd read my book. But the movie is playing. And I see it. And I can't take my eyes off of it. So I end up watching the entire duration of the movie without sound. And at various points throughout it I started welling up, thinking wow, I can't believe I'm crying in a movie I can't hear the sound to. And it's Freaky Friday .
<v Lindsay>所以我在飞机上，我相信是从加州飞往纽约。具体细节没那么突出。但我确实记得它需要2美元作为耳机的押金之类的。我没准备付那2美元，或者我没2美元。我决定读我的书。但电影在播放。我看到了。我无法移开眼睛。所以我最后在没声音的情况下看完了整部电影。在过程中的不同点，我开始涌出泪水，心想：哇，我无法相信我在看一部连声音都听不到的电影时竟然哭了。那部电影是《辣妈辣妹》。

00:50:03.470 --> 00:50:13.760
<v Brett Martin>Or take Steven, an avid film festival goer and professional movie critic who can discourse at length on the differences between early and late period Kurosawa. His plane hadn't even taken off.
<v Brett Martin>或者看看Steven，一位狂热的电影节观众和专业影评人，可以详尽地谈论黑泽明早期和晚期作品的区别。他的飞机甚至还没起飞。

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:43.610
<v Steven>And they were just running this loop of commercials and in-flight programming and stuff. They hadn't started the movie. It was very early on. And there was this AMEX commercial. A man traveling through Europe, and you know, I think it was nighttime. I want to say it was raining or something. And this kind of haggard traveller, this businessman, is walking briskly through the street. And then they close up on a wallet, clearly his, that he had left behind unknowingly.
<v Steven>他们只是在循环播放商业广告和机上节目之类的。他们还没开始放电影。那很早。有个美国运通的广告。一个在欧洲旅行的男人，而且你知道，我想那是晚上。我想说是在下雨之类的。这个有点憔悴的旅行者，这个商人，正匆匆走过街道。然后镜头切换到一个钱包，显然是他的，他不知情地落下了。

00:50:43.610 --> 00:50:59.490
<v Steven>And then you see, cut to the hotel where he's checking in. And the woman asks for a credit card. And then he pats himself down and realizes he doesn't have it. He goes into a state of panic. I think that's when I started choking up.
<v Steven>然后你看到，切到酒店，他在那里办理入住。那个女人要信用卡。然后他摸了摸身上，意识到他没有。他陷入了惊慌。我想那就是我开始哽咽的时候。

00:50:59.490 --> 00:51:31.670
<v Steven>And then he gets American Express on the phone. They explain it'll be OK. He'll have a credit card in the morning. And then I start to relax a little bit. And then he says wait, I'm not going to be in this city. Tomorrow I have to travel. And then I started chocking up again. And then they said, oh, we'll have a waiting for you in that city. And then I just started crying after that. I was so happy for him, and relieved. It was a pretty tense situation there for about 15 or 20 seconds.
<v Steven>然后他接通了美国运通的电话。他们解释说没关系。他明天早上就会有信用卡。然后我开始放松一点。然后他说等等，我不会在这个城市。明天我必须旅行。然后我又开始哽咽了。然后他们说，哦，在那个城市我们会为你准备好。然后我就开始在那之后大哭。我为他感到如此高兴和宽慰。那是一个相当紧张的处境，持续了大约15或20秒。

00:51:31.670 --> 00:52:01.890
<v Brett Martin>This is one of the strange features of our problem. We're less likely to cry at the sad parts of a movie, or financial services industry commercial, than at the happy ones, the parts where everything turns out all right. For instance, in the movie Larger Than Life , which I saw somewhere over the Atlantic a few years ago, it wasn't the moment where Bill Murray is separated from the elephant that his dead circus clown father has left him as a means to change his life as a down-on-his-luck motivational speaker that had me reaching for the tissues. It was when they were reunited.
<v Brett Martin>这是我们这个问题的奇特之处之一。我们不太可能在电影的悲伤部分、或金融服务业广告中哭，而是在快乐的部分，在一切都顺利解决的部分。例如，我在几年前在大西洋上空某处看的电影《超大号的大象》，让我去拿纸巾的不是Bill Murray被迫与那头他死去的马戏团小丑父亲留给他的、作为改变他落魄励志演讲者生活的手段的大象分离的时刻。而是他们重逢的时候。

00:52:01.890 --> 00:52:26.050
<v Brett Martin>In fact, the first time this happened to me was during one of the happiest scenes I'd ever seen. It was in Big Night, Stanley Tucci's movie about fraternal love and Italian food. Mid-way through the movie, Tucci's character and his brother stage a feast in their New Jersey restaurant, and, at one point, bring out a whole roast pig. The camera pans across the faces of the guests just amazed by this unbelievable bounty being wheeled into the room. And the lump because to rise in my throat.
<v Brett Martin>事实上，第一次发生这种情况是在我见过最快乐的场景之一时。那是在《大餐之夜》，Stanley Tucci关于兄弟之爱和意大利食物的电影中。电影进行到一半，Tucci的角色和他的兄弟在他们的新泽西餐厅举办了一场盛宴，在某一时刻，端出一整只烤猪。摄像机扫过客人的脸，他们对这被推进房间的难以置信的慷慨礼物感到惊奇。我喉咙里的肿块涌了起来。

00:52:26.050 --> 00:52:48.060
<v Brett Martin>I found myself brimming over with joy, with the sense that somewhere in the darkness, miles below, just like on screen, people were laughing, communing, sharing a meal. It was impossibly beautiful. And there was just nothing to do but cry.
<v Brett Martin>我发现自己充满了喜悦，意识到在某处黑暗中，几英里之下，就像在屏幕上一样，人们在欢笑、交流、分享一顿饭。它美得不可思议。除了哭泣没别的可做。

00:52:48.060 --> 00:52:54.820
<v Brett Martin>I've never heard of anyone crying inappropriately on trains, or on buses, or in boats, or cars. What is it about airplanes?
<v Brett Martin>我从未听说过有人在火车、公共汽车、船或汽车上不合时宜地哭泣。飞机到底有什么特别之处？

00:52:54.820 --> 00:53:10.830
<v Lindsay>I remember getting off the plane thinking, I should really actually be embarrassed by the fact that I just cried during Freaky Friday and I didn't even hear the sound to it. But I wasn't. It's like, what happens in air stays in air, I guess.
<v Lindsay>我记得下飞机时心想，我真的应该为自己刚才在《辣妈辣妹》期间哭了，甚至连声音都没听到而感到尴尬。但我没有。就像，在空中的事留在空中，我想。

00:53:10.830 --> 00:53:40.100
<v Brett Martin>The people I talked to offered a lot of excuses. It's the recirculated air. Your eyes are dry. You're often tired and leaving people behind. And, of course, there's the obvious conclusion. We're all scared to death. But I've been on hundreds of planes, including quite a few tiny ones, one sea plane that landed on water, and one blimp. I've taken the controls of a plane. I've jumped out of a plane. I've searched my soul. An honest-to-god, I find no fear of flying. And all the frequent criers I interviewed felt the same.
<v Brett Martin>我交谈过的人提供了很多借口。是再循环的空气。你的眼睛干了。你经常很累，把人留在身后。当然，还有显而易见的结论。我们都害怕得要死。但我坐过数百次飞机，包括不少小型飞机，一架在水上着陆的水上飞机，还有一艘飞艇。我握过飞机的操纵杆。我从飞机上跳下来过。我审视过我的灵魂。诚实地说，我发现我不怕坐飞机。而我采访的所有经常在飞机上哭的人也有同样的感受。

00:53:40.100 --> 00:53:58.500
<v Brett Martin>No, something else happens up there, in that weird hanging state between where you're going and where you've left, where there's no phone calls to take, nowhere to go, nothing to do. Some strange overhead compartment of the heart opens up. And critical judgment grabs its flotational seat cushion and follows the lighted pathway to the big, yellow slide.
<v Brett Martin>不，在高处发生了别的事情，在那介于你将要去的地方和已离开的地方之间的奇怪悬空状态中，那里没有电话要接，无处可去，无事可做。心脏的一个奇怪的头顶行李舱打开了。批判性的判断抓起它的浮力垫，沿着照明通道走向那个巨大的黄色滑梯。

00:53:58.500 --> 00:54:10.470
<v Brett Martin>My friend Greg says this actually makes the ride better. Think about it. You're stuck in a seat for 5, or 10, or 15 hours. And how would you rather pass the time? Sitting there being a critic, or just simply giving in?
<v Brett Martin>我的朋友Greg说这实际上让旅程变得更好。想想看。你被困在座位上5、10或15小时。你愿意如何消磨时间？坐在那儿当个批评家，还是干脆屈服？

00:54:10.470 --> 00:54:33.850
<v Greg>I mean, I wouldn't have watched Havana Nights in the waiting area waiting to get on the plane. On Earth, no, not a chance. But once you step on the plane, I'm open to and accepting the movie. And then once you do that, it's going to leave me jelly, turn me into jelly.
<v Greg>我是说，我在等待登机的候机区不会看《哈瓦那之夜》。在地球上，不，绝不可能。但一旦你踏上飞机，我就变得开放并接受这部电影。一旦你那样做了，它就会让我融化，把我变成一滩果冻。

00:54:33.850 --> 00:55:03.660
<v Brett Martin>My own theory goes something like this. My father once told me that the reason squirrels get hit by cars is that evolutionarily nothing in their little hard-wired brains is capable of understanding a large object is hurtling toward them at 70 miles per hour. Well, even though I fly all the time, nothing in my little hard-wired brain is capable of understanding-- I mean really understanding-- stepping onto a metal tube, hanging in space for a while, and then stepping off 6,000 miles away in a place with different weather, different stars, different time.
<v Brett Martin>我自己的理论是这样的。我父亲曾经告诉我，松鼠被汽车撞的原因是进化上它们小小大脑里的任何东西都无法理解一个大物体正以每小时70英里的速度向它们冲来。好吧，尽管我总是坐飞机，但我小小大脑里的任何东西也无法理解——我是说真正理解——走进一个金属管，在空间里悬挂一会儿，然后6000英里外一个有着不同天气、不同星星、不同时间的陌生地方走出来。

00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:16.650
<v Brett Martin>It puts you into a kind of sterile, infantilizing travel purgatory. You're strapped in, given a blanket, a little sippy cup, and tiny silverware, forced to do whatever you're told and borne away at speeds you can't conceive without seeing where you're going.
<v Brett Martin>它把你放进了一种无菌的、幼稚化的旅行炼狱中。你被系着带子，给你一条毯子、一个小吸管杯和微小的餐具，被迫做被告知的任何事，并以你无法想象的速度被带走，看不到你正在去哪里。

00:55:16.650 --> 00:55:53.900
<v Brett Martin>We all deal with this dislocation differently. Many times I've thought, why can't I just have air rage? Why can't I be the guy drinking 14 mini bottles of Amaretto, surfing down the aisle on the dinner cart, groping stewardesses, and cursing? But then, I do a lot of yelling and screaming down here on the ground, even a little groping. What I don't do is cry. Not over breakups, or reunions, or triumphs, or deaths, or leaving home, or coming back, or any of life's other bumps and transformations. Maybe that's the key to my air-- what, sorrow? Maybe I cry the tears I should be shedding on Earth.
<v Brett Martin>我们都以不同的方式处理这种错位。很多次我心想，我为什么不能有“空中狂怒”？为什么我不能是那个喝掉14个小瓶Amaretto、在餐车过道上冲浪、非礼空姐、咒骂的家伙？但话说回来，我在地面上大喊大叫很多，甚至稍微非礼一下。我没做的是哭。不是为了分手、重逢、胜利、死亡、离家或回来，或生活中任何其他的挫折和转变。也许那就是我空中——什么，忧伤？也许我哭的是我应该在地球上洒下的泪水。

00:55:53.900 --> 00:56:17.280
<v Brett Martin>And all of you people who don't cry on airplanes, you're probably the ones I see sobbing on the subway, or on street corners, or at funerals. You probably get it all out at home. Well, boo hoo. Do us all a favor and keep it in the air, you babies.
<v Brett Martin>而你们这些不在飞机上哭的人，你们大概就是我在地铁、街角或葬礼上看到抽泣的人。你们可能在家里就把情绪全释放了。好吧，去哭吧。帮我们个忙，把它留在空中，你们这些婴儿。

00:56:17.280 --> 00:56:31.560
<v Ira Glass>Brett Martin is a correspondent for GQ Magazine in New York.
<v Ira Glass>Brett Martin是纽约《GQ》杂志的通讯员。

